    text(REPORT / 'METHODS_EN.md', f"# Methods and figure caption\n\n## Swedish summarized-data trend\n\nTable1 of Nilsson et al. provided sex-specific birth-rank population sizes and CHD case counts. Adjusted HRs and rounded95%CIs were taken from Tables2–3 without changing their values. Category-specific person-time and adjusted-Cox covariance were not reported. Therefore an exact incidence-rate/HR GLST reconstruction was not possible. At the user's explicit approval, we performed an exploratory generalized-least-squares trend analysis using a cumulative-incidence working covariance reconstruction, not a recovered Swedish Cox model.\n\nFollowing the cumulative-incidence equations in Orsini, Bellocco and Greenland(2006,p46), define R0=1, A0=sum(cases)/sum(Ri Ni/N0), Ai=A0 Ri Ni/N0. Original categoryN and total cases were preserved. We used s0=1/A0−1/N0, si=s0+1/Ai−1/Ni, rhoij=s0/sqrt(si sj), and Vij=SEi SEj rhoij, with diagonal SEi². Published log-HR SEs were [log(UCI)−log(LCI)]/(2×1.95996398454). HRs enter the working risk-ratio equations only to approximate correlation: HR is not generally a cumulative-risk ratio. This extra assumption, as well as preservation of crude-to-adjusted correlation, limits inference. We did not insertN as person-time or manufacture follow-up totals. This follows the published equations and is not claimed to be a literal clone of the current dosresmeta grl(ci) implementation.\n\nCovariance was reconstructed using all five original rank categories, including5+, to retain the published model margins. The primary linear component used only ranks2–4 versus rank1, x=(1,2,3), with no intercept: b=(x'V^-1x)^-1x'V^-1y and Var(b)=(x'V^-1x)^-1. Thus the5+ category required no dose assignment for the primary trend. Male and female slopes were precision-weighted into one Swedish summary, conditionally treating the disjoint strata as independent. Their heterogeneity was recorded; the two sex strata were not presented as independent cohorts. CIs are conditional on the working covariance and do not include all reconstruction uncertainty.\n\nPredeclared sensitivities replaced the correlation with raw cumulative-incidence or raw Poisson case-count correlations and, separately, included5+ at assigned scores5 and6. These scores are scenario assumptions, not observed category means. Residual goodness-of-fit tests assess adequacy of the imposed linear component; a positive slope does not prove monotonicity. Swedish forestN is the full source-modelN=2,673,684; ranks1–4 represent2,587,786 participants. The source HRs were estimated using the full cohorts.\n\n## UKB and LASI\n\nOne additional pooled-sex UKB Cox model was fitted in frozen full-sibships S2–8, restricted to observed absolute ranks1–4. It retained the frozen IHD411 endpoint, complete follow-up, Efron ties, baseline age(linear), sex, official ethnicity groups, assessment center, assessment year, and categoricalS. Current withdrawals were excluded; no missing rank was filled. Actual N={int(uk.N):,}, events={int(uk.events):,}. Ordinary model-based covariance was used. Global and rank-specific proportional-hazards P values were {pph['GLOBAL']:.4g} and {pph['border_rank']:.4g}; these diagnostics are retained rather than resolved by an unrequested model change. The original relative-B analysis was not modified.\n\nPanelB directly reuses the saved UKB DM logistic estimate and LASI DM M1 Firth estimate, with no re-estimation. Both use relative birth position0→1, not a one-rank increment. LASI is diabetes mellitus, not a separately verified T2D endpoint. UKB uses ordinary logistic Wald inference; LASI uses unweighted Firth logistic with profile-likelihoodCI and its frozen age/sex/region/family-size/education/wealth adjustment. The saved UKB inference flag `{dm.inference_status}` is retained; this plotting task does not clear it.\n\nNo UKB–Sweden or UKB–LASI meta-analysis was performed. Swedish trend uncertainty is reconstructed approximately, reference populations and endpoint capture differ, and clinical ascertainment/adjustment differ. PanelA HRs and PanelB ORs also represent different exposure units and are not directly comparable or poolable across panels.\n\n## Figure caption\n\nQuantitative external comparisons of birth-order associations. PanelA shows a pooled-sex UKB Cox HR and a Swedish sex-combined GLST approximation per one absolute birth-rank increment across ranks1–4. The Swedish point is derived from published categorical HRs using a cumulative-incidence working covariance; it is not a directly reported overall Cox HR. PanelB shows saved diabetes-mellitus ORs for relative birth position0→1 in UKB and LASI. Points and bars denote estimates and95%CIs; N denotes source-model sample size. HR andOR panels have distinct units. These are observational quantitative comparisons, not causal validation or pooled cohort effects.\n\nReferences: Nilsson et al., BMJ Open2021;11:e042881, https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2020-042881 ; Greenland and Longnecker1992, https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordjournals.aje.a116237 ; Orsini,Bellocco,Greenland2006, https://doi.org/10.1177/1536867X0600600103 ; Crippa and Orsini2016, https://www.jstatsoft.org/article/view/v072c01 ; Orsini2021, https://doi.org/10.1177/1536867X211025798 .\n")
